Discovery wave 1 complete. I did not score.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Estimated Budgetary Effects of H.R. 5376, the Inflation Reduction Act of 2022","publisher":"Congressional Budget Office","publication_date":"2022-08-03","url":"https://www.cbo.gov/publication/58366","claim":"CBO estimated that enacting the Inflation Reduction Act would reduce federal deficits by $90 billion over 2022-2031.","locator":"Summary, lines 35-37","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent quantitative budget score from the congressional scorekeeper with a direct fiscal estimate.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 legislation and its budget window.","ruler_attribution":"Directly tied to Biden's enacted economic agenda, though the budget effect is shared with Congress and implementation agencies.","contrary_evidence":["CBO later revised the estimate downward after adding spending details.","Budget effects do not by themselves prove household welfare gains."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Additional Information About Increased Enforcement by the Internal Revenue Service","publisher":"Congressional Budget Office","publication_date":"2022-08-25","url":"https://www.cbo.gov/publication/58390","claim":"CBO estimated that increased IRS enforcement funding would raise revenues by $180.4 billion over 2022-2031, with some collections from taxpayers earning under $400,000.","locator":"Summary, lines 38-40","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent budget analysis directly quantifying revenue effects from the IRA's IRS funding.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 enactment and the 2022-2031 budget horizon.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Biden's signing and support for the IRA, but dependent on IRS execution and taxpayer compliance.","contrary_evidence":["CBO says some collections come from taxpayers below $400,000.","This is projected revenue, not realized enforcement outcome."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.5","5B.7"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Statement by Secretary of the Treasury Janet L. Yellen on the Passage of the Inflation Reduction Act of 2022","publisher":"U.S. Department of the Treasury","publication_date":"2022-08-12","url":"https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/jy0920","claim":"Yellen said the IRA would lower health and energy costs, create high-quality jobs, bolster energy security, reduce the deficit, and leave families under $400,000 with no tax increase.","locator":"Paragraph beginning 'Today is an historic day for our country and our economy'","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official administration statement on intended effects and tax design, but it is advocacy rather than independent evaluation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about the August 2022 passage of the IRA.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributed to Biden's legislative agenda and signed bill, though the quoted effects were prospective at the time.","contrary_evidence":["This is administration advocacy, not independent outcome evidence.","Projected benefits had not yet been observed in 2022."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.4","5B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Joint Statement of Janet L. Yellen, Secretary of the Treasury, and Shalanda D. Young, Director of the Office of Management and Budget, on Budget Results for Fiscal Year 2022","publisher":"U.S. Department of the Treasury","publication_date":"2022-10-21","url":"https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/jy1043","claim":"Treasury and OMB said the FY2022 deficit fell by $1.4 trillion, the largest one-year decrease in the federal deficit in American history, to $1.375 trillion.","locator":"Opening paragraph and FY2022 budget summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official fiscal outturn data from Treasury and OMB, though the interpretation is still partly political.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about fiscal year 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to Biden's budget posture and recovery conditions, but strongly mediated by pandemic recovery and revenue swings.","contrary_evidence":["A large part of the improvement reflects cyclical recovery and higher revenues.","Deficit change does not imply broad household welfare improvement."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Biden-Harris Administration Announces CHIPS for America Leadership","publisher":"U.S. Department of Commerce","publication_date":"2022-09-20","url":"https://www.commerce.gov/news/press-releases/2022/09/biden-harris-administration-announces-chips-america-leadership","claim":"Commerce said the CHIPS leadership team included executives experienced in large-scale program management and finance, and that they would implement the CHIPS Act's $50 billion semiconductor investment.","locator":"Paragraph beginning 'The announcements include executives experienced in large-scale program management'","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official implementation announcement showing delegation to professional administrators, but it is still a government press release.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 implementation of a major Biden law.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden's administration appointments and program design choices.","contrary_evidence":["This is a staffing announcement, not a performance record.","Effectiveness still depends on later implementation and outcomes."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.6"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"FACT SHEET: The Biden-Harris Plan to Revitalize American Manufacturing and Secure Critical Supply Chains in 2022","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-02-24","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fact-sheet-the-biden-harris-plan-revitalize-american-manufacturing-and-secure-critical","claim":"The Biden administration said it had completed supply-chain reviews, created a Supply Chain Disruptions Task Force, and argued those actions contributed to a historic manufacturing recovery and 367,000 manufacturing jobs in Biden's first year.","locator":"Lines 63-68","source_type":"other","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Secondary transcript of an official White House fact sheet, useful but not as strong as the original White House page.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Policy and narrative context leading into 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributed to Biden's first-year executive actions and industrial strategy, though the jobs number also reflects broader recovery.","contrary_evidence":["The jobs figure is administration framing and overlaps with post-pandemic rebound.","Claim is about first-year context, not a measured 2022-only effect."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.7","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"FACT SHEET: Executive Order on Promoting Competition in the American Economy","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2021-07-09","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fact-sheet-executive-order-promoting-competition-the-american-economy","claim":"Biden's competition order was framed as a whole-of-government effort with 72 initiatives to lower prices, raise wages, promote innovation, and reduce corporate consolidation.","locator":"Lines 62-72","source_type":"other","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Secondary transcript of an official White House fact sheet; strong for policy intent, weaker for downstream effects.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Signed before 2022 but active as inherited policy context during 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden's executive action, though this is intent and design rather than outcome evidence.","contrary_evidence":["This does not show that prices fell because of the order.","Competition policy effects usually materialize slowly and with confounding factors."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.4","5B.5"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Consumer Price Index News Release - 2022 M12 Results","publisher":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics","publication_date":"2023-01-12","url":"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/cpi_01122023.htm","claim":"The CPI-U increased 6.5 percent over the 12 months ending December 2022, the smallest 12-month increase since October 2021.","locator":"Lines 226-230 and 387-389","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary federal inflation statistic with direct annual measurement.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly measures 2022 inflation conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly context and shock response rather than direct presidential control, because inflation is driven by many domestic and global factors.","contrary_evidence":["Monetary policy is independent of the president.","High inflation still indicates weak household purchasing power in 2022."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Gross Domestic Product, Fourth Quarter and Year 2022 (Second Estimate)","publisher":"U.S. Bureau of Economic Analysis","publication_date":"2023-03-30","url":"https://www.bea.gov/news/2023/gross-domestic-product-fourth-quarter-and-year-2022-second-estimate","claim":"Real GDP increased 2.1 percent in 2022, while the PCE price index increased 6.3 percent.","locator":"Lines 112-114","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary national accounts release from BEA with direct annual output and price data.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 national output and inflation.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to policy mix and recovery conditions, but not to the president alone.","contrary_evidence":["Inflation remained high even with GDP growth.","GDP growth does not capture distribution or household purchasing power."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Personal Income and Outlays, December 2022","publisher":"U.S. Bureau of Economic Analysis","publication_date":"2023-01-27","url":"https://www.bea.gov/news/2023/personal-income-and-outlays-december-2022","claim":"BEA reported that the PCE price index was 5.0 percent higher than a year earlier in December 2022, and real PCE fell 0.3 percent in the month.","locator":"Lines 106-108","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary federal income-and-spending release with direct household-side macro data.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers late-2022 household purchasing power.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly contextual, because this reflects broader inflation dynamics rather than a ruler-specific decision alone.","contrary_evidence":["Monthly data are volatile and not all attributable to Biden.","Real spending can fall even when longer-term income trends improve."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Federal Reserve issues FOMC statement","publisher":"Federal Reserve Board","publication_date":"2022-03-16","url":"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20220316a.htm","claim":"The Fed raised the federal funds target range to 0.25-0.50 percent and said inflation remained elevated due to supply and demand imbalances, energy prices, and broader price pressures.","locator":"Lines 312-316","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary central bank policy statement on the inflation environment and policy response.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly situates 2022 inflation and monetary tightening.","ruler_attribution":"This is independent of Biden, which matters for separating presidential control from macro outcomes.","contrary_evidence":["Monetary policy is not under direct presidential control.","The statement does not assess Biden's policy quality directly."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Speech by Chair Powell on monetary policy and price stability","publisher":"Federal Reserve Board","publication_date":"2022-08-26","url":"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/speech/powell20220826a.htm","claim":"Powell said the Fed's focus was to bring inflation back to its 2 percent goal and that it was taking forceful and rapid steps to moderate demand and anchor expectations.","locator":"Speech opening paragraphs on monetary policy and price stability","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary speech by the Fed chair on the inflation-fighting stance and policy objective.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 inflation stabilization conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Independent of the president, useful for separating presidential policy from Fed action.","contrary_evidence":["The president does not set Fed policy.","Aggressive tightening can slow growth, so it is not a pure prosperity indicator."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The US Economy's Inflation Challenge","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-07-12","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/07/11/cf-us-economy-inflation-challenge","claim":"The IMF said the U.S. demand rebound stressed supply chains and caused inflation to rise sharply, with wages for most workers failing to keep up and eroding purchasing power.","locator":"Lines 282-284","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent international institution summarizing macro conditions and household purchasing-power pressure.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about mid-2022 macro conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly contextual rather than Biden-specific, because the IMF attributes much of the inflation to demand, supply-chain, and global price shocks.","contrary_evidence":["This is not a ruler-specific judgment.","Global events and Federal Reserve policy are major confounders."],"lenses":["5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"United States: 2022 Article IV Consultation-Press Release; Staff Report; and Statement by the Executive Director for the United States","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-07-12","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/publications/cr/issues/2022/07/12/united-states-2022-article-iv-consultation-press-release-staff-report-and-statement-by-the-520659","claim":"The IMF said unemployment and other measures of labor force underutilization had returned to end-2019 levels and output was close to its pre-pandemic trend.","locator":"Lines 260-260 in the summary text","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent country review with direct macro assessment and explicit labor-market benchmark comparison.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly relevant to 2022 macro trajectory and recovery status.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to the administration's policy mix, but also to the post-pandemic rebound and Fed actions.","contrary_evidence":["The same report emphasizes the Fed's tightening path.","Labor-market recovery does not resolve inflation or distribution concerns."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Employment Situation - December 2022","publisher":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics","publication_date":"2023-01-06","url":"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_01062023.pdf","claim":"BLS reported that nonfarm payroll employment rose by 223,000 in December 2022, the unemployment rate edged down to 3.5 percent, and payroll employment rose by 4.5 million over 2022.","locator":"Page 1, lines 6-9 and page 2, lines 74-77","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary monthly labor-market release with direct employment and unemployment statistics.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the final month of 2022 and the year's labor-market gains.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly contextual, since labor-market outcomes reflect broad recovery and policy mix rather than a single president alone.","contrary_evidence":["Payroll gains do not isolate presidential causation.","Sectoral gains still left some industries below prepandemic employment."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Unemployment rate returned to its prepandemic level in 2022","publisher":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.bls.gov/opub/mlr/2023/article/unemployment-rate-returned-to-its-prepandemic-level-in-2022.htm","claim":"BLS found that in the fourth quarter of 2022 the unemployment rate averaged 3.6 percent, 0.6 percentage point below a year earlier, and returned to its prepandemic level, while labor force participation remained below prepandemic.","locator":"Introductory summary and quarterly labor-force discussion","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"BLS synthesis article using CPS and CES data to contextualize the 2022 labor recovery.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about the 2022 labor market.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual rather than directly presidential; the article describes labor-market conditions, not Biden-specific causation.","contrary_evidence":["Labor-force participation still lagged prepandemic levels.","This does not show whether the improvement was due to Biden policy."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Poverty in the United States: 2022","publisher":"U.S. Census Bureau","publication_date":"2023-09-12","url":"https://www.census.gov/library/publications/2023/demo/p60-280.html","claim":"Census reported that the official poverty rate in 2022 was 11.5 percent, unchanged from 2021, while the Supplemental Poverty Measure rose to 12.4 percent and child SPM poverty more than doubled from 5.2 percent to 12.4 percent.","locator":"Highlights section, lines 211-216","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary poverty release with direct household welfare measures and explicit 2022 comparison.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about household conditions in calendar year 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Partly shaped by the expiration of pandemic relief and the inflation environment, so Biden attribution is mixed and not exclusive.","contrary_evidence":["The official poverty rate did not worsen, even though the SPM did.","This reflects tax policy expiration as well as administration policy."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Income in the United States: 2022","publisher":"U.S. Census Bureau","publication_date":"2023-09-12","url":"https://www.census.gov/data/tables/2023/demo/income-poverty/p60-279.html","claim":"Census reported real median household income of $74,580 in 2022, down 2.3 percent from 2021, while the money income Gini index fell from 0.494 to 0.488, its first annual decrease since 2007, and 65.6 percent of working women worked full-time year-round.","locator":"Highlights section, lines 185-246","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary national income report with direct distribution and earnings measures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly measures 2022 household income and inequality.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly contextual because household income and inequality are influenced by inflation, labor markets, transfers, and tax policy together.","contrary_evidence":["Median income fell despite employment strength.","Distribution improved on one measure even as other household-welfare metrics worsened."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"OECD Economic Surveys: United States 2022","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-10-12","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-surveys-united-states-2022_eeb7cbe9-en.html","claim":"The OECD said the U.S. economy rebounded strongly due to a large and enduring policy response, but Russia's war against Ukraine and inflation pressures dampened the outlook, while the administration was reinforcing public welfare through infrastructure and climate packages and the middle class faced stagnating incomes and rising costs.","locator":"Executive summary and special feature on the middle class","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent cross-country policy assessment with explicit discussion of U.S. policy, inflation, and middle-class pressure.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about the 2022 policy and macro context.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to Biden's policy agenda, but the OECD also emphasizes global shocks and longer-run structural trends.","contrary_evidence":["The report is not a score of Biden personally.","It stresses preexisting middle-class stagnation and global inflation shocks."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IRS Inflation Reduction Act Strategic Operating Plan","publisher":"Internal Revenue Service","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://www.irs.gov/about-irs/irs-inflation-reduction-act-strategic-operating-plan","claim":"The IRS said IRA funding would improve taxpayer service, update technology, invest in employees with new tools and capabilities, and allow effective enforcement of tax laws.","locator":"Summary paragraphs under Fiscal years 2023-2031","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official implementation plan, strong on intended administrative use of funds but not on realized outcomes yet.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about the 2022 IRA implementation plan.","ruler_attribution":"Directly connected to Biden's tax-administration agenda and to delegated executive implementation.","contrary_evidence":["This is an operating plan, not a completed performance record.","Benefits depend on later staffing, technology, and enforcement execution."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.5","5B.7"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Credits and deductions under the Inflation Reduction Act of 2022","publisher":"Internal Revenue Service","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://www.irs.gov/credits-and-deductions-under-the-inflation-reduction-act-of-2022","claim":"IRS said it was implementing the IRA and listed new or expanded credits for clean vehicles, home energy, and business clean-energy investment.","locator":"Lines 297-360","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official taxpayer guidance showing the law's concrete policy channels, though it is still implementation guidance rather than outcomes.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 law implementation and its tax provisions.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden's enacted legislation, with effects mediated by taxpayer uptake and IRS administration.","contrary_evidence":["Guidance availability does not show uptake or welfare gains.","Some benefits were delayed until future tax years."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.4","5B.6"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"U.S. Department of Energy Issues Comprehensive Plan to Strengthen America's Clean Energy Supply Chains and Bolster Domestic Manufacturing","publisher":"U.S. Department of Energy","publication_date":"2022-02-24","url":"https://www.energy.gov/cmei/solar/articles/us-department-energy-issues-comprehensive-plan-strengthen-americas-clean-energy","claim":"DOE released seven deep-dive assessments of clean-energy supply chains, saying they would help the Biden administration's net-zero 2050 goal and create opportunities for new jobs and manufacturing infrastructure.","locator":"Lines 159-170","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official agency supply-chain assessment linking policy design to industrial capacity and jobs, though still prospective.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 industrial-policy planning.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Biden administration policy choices, but the evidence is planning-oriented rather than outcome-based.","contrary_evidence":["The report is forward-looking, not a realized outcome measure.","Supply-chain improvements may take years and may be confounded by private investment cycles."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Fact Sheet: Equity in the Bipartisan Infrastructure Law","publisher":"U.S. Department of Transportation","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://www.transportation.gov/bipartisan-infrastructure-law/fact-sheet-equity-bipartisan-infrastructure-law","claim":"DOT said the Bipartisan Infrastructure Law would connect disadvantaged communities to jobs and economic opportunities, make the largest transit investment ever, and create good-paying jobs across the country.","locator":"Lines 124-149","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official implementation fact sheet showing intended distributional and productivity effects, but not outcome realization.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 infrastructure implementation.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden's signature legislation, though the law itself is shared with Congress and future administrators.","contrary_evidence":["This is program intent, not realized distributional outcome.","Infrastructure effects are slow-moving and regionally uneven."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8"]}
Search ledger: candidates found about 38, documents opened 24, duplicates or near-duplicates collapsed 10, irrelevant or out-of-period items 4, inaccessible items 3, accepted distinct URLs 23.

Material gaps and next best directions:
- Direct, ruler-specific appointment evidence is still thin. Next best sources: Treasury, OMB, Commerce, and NEC nomination/confirmation records for 2022, plus any official bios showing delegated authority.
- Distributional evidence for 2022 is still mostly aggregate. Next best sources: BEA distribution-of-personal-income state files, Census post-tax tables, BLS wage percentiles, and CRS/CBPP analysis of the 2022 poverty and Child Tax Credit reversal.
- There is still limited outcome evidence tying Biden's specific actions to price relief in 2022. Next best sources: CBO, IRS, and BEA follow-through on IRA provisions; Reuters/AP on CHIPS and industrial investment announcements; and empirical work on inflation, real wages, and investment response.
- Regional and household heterogeneity is still underdeveloped. Next best sources: BEA state/county income distribution, Census SAIPE, and BLS metro/state wage tables.
